`postCheckRunStampRetryOnce` drops the second attempt's error object: on a persistent failure the caller only receives `false`. So the tick log reads:

```
⚠️  stamp "Quality gate" on 1118caf failed (exit 2: …) — retrying once
```

…and nothing more for this stamp until the tick-end `stamps_failed: N` counter and the 🚨 absence alarm — which surface the count, but not the cause. An operator scanning the per-stamp warns can read the `— retrying once` line as a recovery. The final error can also differ from the first (e.g. transient network, then an auth/quota failure), and that distinguishing signal is lost.

Suggested shape — pass the attempt index to `onRetry` and let the SM callback phrase the last attempt differently:

```js
// js/common/checkRunStamp.js
} catch (eStamp) {
    if (typeof onRetry === 'function') onRetry(eStamp, attempt);
}
```

```js
